  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 338 PM EDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nSaturday Night - Tuesday Night...\n\nThe closed low is expected to cut off from the mean upper flow, and \nthen meander very slowly eastward through the 2nd part of the \nweekend and into the first part of next week. The bulk of model data \nsuggests this feature could hang around our region through Tuesday \nbefore getting picked up by the stronger westerlies associated with \na digging north central CONUS trough. The main impact on sensible \nweather is expected to be lingering showers and a few thunderstorms, \nmainly each afternoon and evening, however will have to carry some \nisolated chances each night as well given the high moisture content \nwrapped up in this system (PWATs around 2\"). Given that the best \ndeep layer shear will move off to the east, expect fairly slow storm \nmovement late weekend/early week. A few isolated flooding issues \ncould arise, or pre-existing flooding issues from Saturday may be \nexacerbated. Expect below normal temperatures (upper 70s and low \n80s) under fairly heavy sky cover, and smaller diurnal ranges with \novernight lows mostly in the mid to upper 60s.\n\nWednesday - Friday...\n\nThings will trend warmer for the late week time frame, although \nthere will also likely be a dry period (Wed/Thur is best bet) in \nthis stretch. This will occur as we sit on the southern fringes of a \ndigging north central CONUS trough, and on the northern periphery of \nan upper ridge. Forecast models loosely suggest an impinging weak \nfrontal boundary trailing from an eastern Canada system or a weak \nembedded shortwave could rotate into the area by Friday. Given our \nincreased heat and humidity, plenty of instability should be present \nfor shower and storm development. Low confidence on timing or any \nindividual feature, but in general chances for showers and storms \nshould increase into the first part of the weekend. Looking for mid \n80s by Wednesday, and highs peaking in the upper 80s and low 90s by \nThur/Fri.",
